# Slice 416 (the author, 2026-11-11, answer 6, LMX_blog/q/colon-declares-answered-20261111.md; docs/LMX_semantics.en.md
# :642-643, :681): of the two operands of `call` the first is the data and the second the code, in `call(D C)` and
# `call: D C` alike, and the operands are evaluated in the written order.  Every code and every operand writes its own
# digit into the root's `log` by an explicit path, so the value names which Structure ran as the code and in which
# order the operands were evaluated.  Entry 7; 81.. name the failing check.
int: log 0
A:
    node\log := node\log * 10 + 1
B:
    node\log := node\log * 10 + 2
Data1:
    int: v 1
Code1:
    node\log := node\log * 10 + 3
fn: first () Data1
    node\log := node\log * 10 + 1
    return: Data1
fn: second () Code1
    node\log := node\log * 10 + 2
    return: Code1
# 81: `call(A B)` runs B over A.
fn: roles () int
    call(A B)
    return: node\log
# 82: the colon form, `call: B A` runs A over B.
fn: roles_colon () int
    call: B A
    return: node\log
# 83: the data first() is evaluated before the code second(); then Code1 runs.
fn: order () int
    call(first() second())
    return: node\log
int: code 7
if: roles() != 2
    code := 81
if: code = 7 && roles_colon() != 21
    code := 82
if: code = 7 && order() != 21123
    code := 83
send: exit(exit_code: code; stdout: ""; stderr: "")
return
